<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Wycliffe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1328–<a href="/years/1384">1384</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as John Wyclif</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Wycliffe was an English theologian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> scholar who attacked the wealth and authority of the Church, questioned transubstantiation and held that Scripture was the supreme authority. He inspired the first complete translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> into English and the Lollard movement. His writings reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> and strongly influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hus">Jan Hus</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> condemned him as a heretic in 1415.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-wycliffe.jpg" alt="John Wycliffe" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Kirkby · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wycliffe_by_Kirby.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Wycliffe was born in the village of Hipswell, near Richmond in the North Riding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>, though the date is disputed: it is conventionally given as 1324, while records suggest the mid-1320s or, according to one scholar, a date after 1331. He is known to have been at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> around 1345. The book On the Cause of God Against the Pelagians by Thomas Bradwardine shaped his views, as did the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>, which reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in the summer of 1348 and which he mentioned often in later life. He became a priest in September 1351 and completed his bachelor of arts at Merton College in 1356. In 1361 he was Master of Balliol College.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1374 Wycliffe was part of a group that negotiated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> for the English government with the envoys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xi">Pope Gregory XI</a>, which may have begun his connection with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a>, the Duke of Lancaster. He then began to write tracts and longer works. In De civili dominio he attacked the temporal rule of the clergy, the collection of annates, indulgences and simony. His theory of dominion held that the Church should not own property and that men in mortal sin could not exercise authority in Church or state, and he insisted on the poverty of all clergy.</p><p>In the summer of 1381 he set out his doctrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Lord's Supper</a> in twelve short sentences, and the chancellor of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> had some of them pronounced heretical. Wycliffe appealed to the king. Once he dismissed the doctrine of transubstantiation, he lost the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a> and many others. In his last years he argued that Scripture was the authoritative centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, that the claims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> were unhistorical, and that monasticism was corrupt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Wycliffe withdrew to Lutterworth, where he sent out tracts against the monks and Pope Urban VI and left his last work, the Opus evangelicum, unfinished. He suffered a stroke while saying Mass on 28 December 1384 and died a few days later. Tradition credits him with a Middle English translation of the Vulgate Bible, now called Wycliffe's Bible, completed before 1384 and revised by John Purvey in 1388 and 1395, but more recent scholars suggest he had at most a minor part. His followers, called Lollards by their opponents, adopted many of his views, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> writings greatly influenced the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hus">Jan Hus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Wycliffe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jan Hus, a Prague preacher influenced by the writings of <b>John Wycliffe</b>, attacked church abuses and was burned at the Council of Constance in 1415.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Hussitism organised itself in the years 1415 to 1419, influenced by the teachings of <b>John Wycliffe</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/hussites">Hussites · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The followers of <b>John Wycliffe</b> in England were driven underground, and Jan Hus was burned as a heretic at the Council of Constance in 1415, although his followers kept a church of their own in Bohemia.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hipswell, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Lutterworth, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.38,-1.72&amp;year=1364" aria-label="Hipswell on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John Wycliffe"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4914 5479h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4914 5479h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.38,-1.72&amp;year=1364">What was here in 1364? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
